      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n- **II.** $D$ is a singular matrix\n\n- **III.** $det(D)=- \\frac{5}{21}$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & 4 & \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{3} & -2 & \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & 4 & \\frac{2}{7} \\\\ 0 & -1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{3} & -2 & \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ -2 & \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}|+(-4)|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{7} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}|+\\frac{2}{7}|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ - \\frac{1}{3} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(- \\frac{1}{7}+\\frac{2}{7})+(-4)(0+\\frac{1}{21})+\\frac{2}{7}(0- \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{21}+\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot (- \\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=- \\frac{5}{21}\\ne 0$, the matrix is **not** singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
